# Keybed Voltage Funk

Ingredients: JAPANESE TAISHŌGOTO + MINNEAPOLIS ELECTRO-FUNK + ANALOG POLYSYNTH + LINN-STYLE DRUM MACHINE + ELECTRIC BASS + MUTED RHYTHM GUITAR + BASS CLARINET

Creative Catalyst: The genre began during a late-summer residency in a converted municipal typewriter-repair annex in Nagoya, where taishōgoto player Rei Matsuda was testing short keyed-zither phrases while Minneapolis producer Simone Avery rebuilt dry electro-funk grooves from drum machine, electric bass, clipped guitar, and bright analog polysynth chords. Their first attempt treated the taishōgoto as a novelty melody instrument over an already complete funk arrangement. The useful discovery came when Simone isolated the mechanical key noise from Rei’s instrument and noticed that every pitch change produced a tiny percussive event before the string fully rang. She stopped filtering those noises out and instead aligned selected drum-machine ghost hits to the physical key presses. The taishōgoto became both melody and micro-percussion: a line could articulate pitch with one layer while its buttons supplied a second rhythmic skeleton. The band then designed the rest of the groove around the difference between keyed attack, plucked-string decay, and machine backbeat.

Keybed Voltage Funk is a dry, taut acoustic-electric dance style built from the mechanically keyed pluck of the Japanese taishōgoto and the clipped architecture of late-70s/80s Minneapolis electro-funk. It normally runs between 108 and 122 BPM in 4/4. The rhythm section uses compact Linn-style kick and snare, narrow clap layers, precise hi-hat, syncopated electric bass, muted rhythm guitar, and bright but controlled analog-polysynth voicings. The taishōgoto occupies the upper-mid register with short one- to six-note cells, repeated-note riffs, chromatic pickups, octave jumps, and deliberate key-click accents.

The core aesthetic is mechanical precision without removing the hand. The taishōgoto’s numbered or keyed pitch mechanism creates a distinctive separation between action and tone: the player depresses a key, the mechanism contacts the string, and the plectrum or pick excites the note. That tiny sequence becomes compositional material. Key noise is not treated as contamination. It is one of the genre’s principal rhythmic layers.

The taishōgoto is recorded with one small-diaphragm condenser aimed toward the bridge/string area, one close dynamic or contact microphone near the key mechanism, and one ribbon microphone 60–90 cm back for body warmth. The key microphone is high-passed so it captures clicks, button impact, and mechanical release without adding low handling noise. The bridge microphone carries pitch definition. The ribbon provides body and prevents the instrument from sounding thin beside synths.

Players favor minor pentatonic figures, dominant-seventh fragments, chromatic approach notes, open fifths, repeated roots, octave displacement, major-sixth color, and compact minor-third cells. Continuous arpeggiation is avoided. The instrument works best when the line feels punched into the arrangement one key event at a time.

The primary compositional device is the Key Contact. A taishōgoto pitch change is deliberately written so the physical key press becomes part of the groove. Some key clicks align with hats or guitar scratches; others replace them. The note itself may land directly on the beat or fractionally after the mechanical click, creating a microscopic two-stage attack.

A second device, Split Trigger, separates the key noise from the pitched string in the arrangement. During one phrase, the key click may be reinforced by a machine rimshot while the string note remains dry. During another, the pitched note may be doubled by synth while the click is left exposed. The player therefore generates two independently orchestrated events with one gesture.

A third device, Voltage Stair, builds riffs from stepwise pitch movement whose timbral density increases with register. Low taishōgoto notes are paired only with bass or kick; middle notes may add guitar; upper notes may authorize synth stabs or clap reinforcement. A rising four-note line can therefore make the entire arrangement brighten without using filter automation or a conventional build.

A fourth device, Release Gap, leaves one sixteenth or eighth note of deliberate silence after selected taishōgoto notes so the mechanical key release is audible. Drums may continue, but bass, guitar, and keys withdraw from that subdivision. This turns the physical return of the key into a rhythmic punctuation mark.

A fifth device, False Unison, pairs taishōgoto and analog synth on the same written pitch but gives them opposite articulation. The taishōgoto uses a short dry pluck while the synth uses a soft swell, or the synth stabs while the taishōgoto lets the string ring. The ear perceives unison harmony but contrasting time envelopes.

Drums remain disciplined and compact. Kick is short, dry, and mid-forward. Snare is crisp with a narrow gated room or short plate. Claps are small and tightly layered. Hi-hats use straight or lightly swung sixteenths with velocity hierarchy rather than trap-style rolls. Percussion is sparse enough for key noise to remain audible.

Electric bass is fingerstyle or tightly muted pick style. Lines use root, fifth, sixth, flat seventh, octave, chromatic pickups, and short anticipations. Bass is active but leaves windows for the taishōgoto. Sustained sub-bass and long glide notes are excluded.

Muted electric guitar supplies scratches, two-note chord fragments, high-register sixths, and clipped offbeat stabs. Chorus is restrained. The guitar never doubles the taishōgoto continuously; it functions as a dry midrange counter-rhythm.

Analog polysynth provides bright, controlled harmonic color through major 7, minor 7, dominant 9, sus2, add9, major 6, and quartal voicings. Short brass-like stabs are allowed, but no giant retro pad wash. Filter envelopes should be quick and articulate.

Bass clarinet provides low acoustic contrast in bridges and breakdowns. It uses dry chalumeau stabs, key clicks, breath attacks, short falls, and occasional slap-tongue. Its mechanical key noise creates a subtle acoustic mirror of the taishōgoto mechanism without copying the melody.

Vocals are optional but useful. Japanese and English can coexist in clipped phrases, deadpan hooks, low unison refrains, or dry call-and-response. Lyrics work best around buttons, ticket machines, repair counters, late trains, fluorescent rooms, receipts, hand-written numbers, and physical objects that respond only after pressure is applied.

Effects remain short and specific. Taishōgoto gets a compact room and tiny plate; key noise stays nearly dry. Synth may use mild chorus and short stereo delay. Guitar uses a small amp room. Bass clarinet gets a dark chamber. Avoid synthwave wash, EDM risers, trap rolls, cinematic Japanese textures, fake koto patches, and oversized gated drums.

The defining tension is between a human finger pressing a physical key and an electronic rhythm section that appears machine-perfect. The more exact the beat becomes, the more audible the hand mechanism must remain.

## Genre DNA

Tempo & Pulse: 108–122 BPM, 4/4 Minneapolis-style electro-funk with Linn-like kick/snare, tight sixteenth hats, syncopated electric bass, clipped guitar, bright analog-synth punctuation, and taishōgoto key-click micro-rhythm.

Harmonic Palette: Minor pentatonic, dominant 7 and 9, major 6, minor 7, sus2, add9, open fifths, octave figures, chromatic pickups, repeated roots, and compact quartal synth voicings.

Production Signatures:
1. Key Contact — physical taishōgoto key presses are retained and rhythmically orchestrated instead of edited out.
2. Split Trigger — one taishōgoto gesture generates two separately arranged layers: key click and pitched string.
3. Voltage Stair — register changes authorize progressively brighter or denser ensemble responses.
4. Release Gap — small arrangement vacancies expose the mechanical release of selected keys.
5. False Unison — taishōgoto and synth share pitch but use opposite articulation envelopes.
6. No-Novelty-Zither Rule — the taishōgoto must hold a structural rhythm/melody role throughout the track and cannot be reduced to an intro effect or decorative solo.

Dynamics: Intensity grows through denser key-click reinforcement, busier bass anticipations, higher Voltage Stair registers, brighter synth stabs, tighter guitar scratches, and more frequent False Unisons. Peaks remain lean, dry, and dance-functional rather than huge.

# Scene & Origin Lore

Keybed Voltage Funk formed inside a former municipal typewriter, adding-machine, and ticket-printer repair annex in Nagoya. The building retained metal parts drawers, numbered service tags, fluorescent inspection lamps, keycap trays, rubber rollers, carbon-paper boxes, manual receipt printers, workbenches, soldering irons, and narrow windows overlooking an elevated rail line.

Taishōgoto player Rei Matsuda had been using the annex because its dry surfaces made the instrument’s key mechanism unusually audible. Her background crossed community taishōgoto ensemble playing, experimental chamber music, and rhythm-focused improvisation. She became interested in the fact that every pitch change created a small mechanical sound before or alongside the musical note.

Minneapolis producer Simone Avery arrived with a Linn-style drum machine, analog polysynth, direct-recorded bass, and a set of unfinished electro-funk charts. Her first version layered Rei over a complete arrangement and left no reason for the mechanical instrument to exist. During a rehearsal, the bridge microphone failed but the key-mechanism mic stayed live. Simone heard an entire secondary rhythm in the raw button presses and rebuilt the drum pattern around it.

Bassist Kenji Sato joined after developing short syncopated lines that leave deliberate Release Gaps. Guitarist Leah Price reduced her part to muted sixteenths, dry chord fragments, and upper-register sixths. Bass-clarinetist Arata Nishimura completed the ensemble by adding low mechanical key noise and breath punctuation in sections where another synthesizer would have made the texture too electronic.

Their first performances were called service counters. A small illuminated board behind Rei displayed KEY, STRING, or BOTH depending on which part of the taishōgoto gesture carried structural priority in the section. The board controlled nothing. It simply made the dual-layer concept visible.

The visual language developed around chrome grey, tatami straw beige, receipt white, carbon-paper blue, signal red, black plastic keycaps, fluorescent green, and warm walnut. Wardrobe favors work shirts, knit tops, loose trousers, simple dresses, low-profile sneakers, and practical contemporary clothing rather than retro cosplay or ceremonial styling.

The unofficial rule is that if the taishōgoto can be replaced by a generic pluck synth without changing the groove architecture, the arrangement has failed.

