# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Key 14 Before the Snare

Story & Context: At 116 BPM, Simone opens with a dry Linn-style kick, crisp snare, closed sixteenth hats, and no synth chord. Rei enters with a four-note taishōgoto cell whose second key press lands just ahead of beat two. The physical click appears first; the string note blooms a fraction later; the snare lands immediately after. This three-part event becomes the hook. Kenji adds a short root-sixth-octave bass phrase only after the taishōgoto finishes. Leah answers with one muted guitar sixth. In the chorus, Split Trigger is used explicitly: selected key clicks are doubled by rimshot while the pitched notes receive soft analog-synth False Unisons.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Close-up of a numbered service key marked 14 beside a chrome typewriter carriage and a taishōgoto string, red inspection tag hanging from the bench, fluorescent reflections on worn metal. Sparse lyric fragments: “key fourteen,” “before the snare,” “press then sound,” “まだ鳴る / still ringing.”

## Track 2: Carbon Copy Has a Different Groove

Story & Context: At 122 BPM, the EP’s tightest track begins with a Voltage Stair riff moving from low tonic to middle fifth to upper minor third to octave. Each register adds one new layer: bass, muted guitar, clap, then synth stab. The chord never changes, but the arrangement appears to climb with the instrument. Midway through the track, Rei repeats the exact pitch sequence while Simone reverses the density map, creating a second “carbon copy” whose groove feels different despite identical notes. Release Gaps expose key return noises at the end of each four-bar phrase. Arata adds short bass-clarinet key clicks and chalumeau stabs without doubling the melody.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Two overlapping carbon-paper receipts with identical numbers but different handwritten timing marks, loose black keycaps, blue carbon smudges, taishōgoto pick on a chrome repair tray. Sparse lyric fragments: “carbon copy,” “same number,” “different groove,” “押した跡 / the mark from pressing.”

## Track 3: Fluorescent Line After the Last Train

Story & Context: The closer runs at 108 BPM and uses the sparsest rhythm on the EP. Kick appears only on selected beats, snare remains dry, and hats are reduced to uneven eighths. Rei plays longer taishōgoto notes than elsewhere, allowing the string to ring while the key mechanism remains still. Simone uses soft minor-7 and add9 synth voicings in False Unison with opposite envelopes: when Rei plucks short, the synth swells; when Rei lets the note ring, the synth becomes a brief stab. In the final minute, bass and guitar drop out. The key microphone becomes more prominent until the last musical note ends. Rei presses one final silent key without plucking the string. The EP ends on the mechanical click and spring return alone.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Empty elevated-platform service corridor after the last train, one fluorescent tube still lit, taishōgoto resting on a workbench beside a receipt printer, a single loose keycap near the edge, distant rail lights outside. Sparse lyric fragments: “last train gone,” “fluorescent line,” “press without sound,” “帰る前に / before going home.”

